v0.43.27 - Air Lock supply channels attach independently to all hull sides
v0.43.26 - failsafe reset control; smooth solar tracker slew; light-yellow selection outlines; oxygen generator throughput +100%
0.43.15 - hydrogen fuel-cell generation and tracked stellar solar input
- Add Hydrogen Generator as an outdoor buildable resource structure with native procedural fuel-cell/radiator/manifold geometry, construction costs, catalog icon, selection telemetry and supply warnings.
- Oxygen Generator electrolysis now preserves the physical 1:8 H2:O2 by-product mass ratio. Each generator buffers size-scaled hydrogen locally; excess is reported as vented when the buffer is full. Hydrogen inventory is persistent in save schema 20; schemas 1-19 remain readable and default to an empty H2 buffer.
- Connected Hydrogen Generators consume H2 only from Oxygen Generators in the same completed Supply Channel component. Net electrical output uses 33.3 kWh/kg hydrogen LHV at 55% fuel-cell electrical efficiency (18.315 kWh/kg net) and is demand-dispatched: local loads first, then connected batteries, with no fuel burn when neither can use the energy.
- Replace the old scalar sunrise/sunset sine factor with a reusable 3D stellar pose. The current nonvisualized reference follows an equinox-like 30-degree-north arc: east sunrise, southern ~60-degree noon altitude, west sunset and below-horizon night, with a stable future render position.
- Completed Solar Panels use a two-axis collector-head transform that follows the hidden star while keeping the pedestal fixed. With an airless environment and normal-incidence tracking, rated photovoltaic output is retained while the star is above the horizon apart from a narrow 2-degree horizon ramp.
- Extend UI and regressions for H2 production/buffering/dispatch, network isolation, save persistence, star motion and physical panel tracking. Existing Stage 1-3 progression and robot/build behavior remain unchanged.

0.43.14 - life-support consumption and colony progression
- Keep the existing component-local utility simulation and expose explicit electrical, water and oxygen demand. Fresh colonies begin uncrewed; completed Crew Quarters provide 2/4/6/8 crew capacity for Small/Standard/Mid/Big sizes. Existing saves remain readable and keep any larger already-populated crew capacity.
- Use the existing realistic crew needs of 0.84 kg O2 and 4 L water per person/game-day as named life-support constants. Fully pressurized interiors (>=99%) now lose a conservative 0.1% of contained oxygen per game-day through seals/hatches, so the oxygen generator must continue consuming network water and power to maintain a base.
- Show actual POWER load and WATER use alongside signed battery/tank net rates. Demand remains visible during shortages instead of collapsing to only the amount successfully supplied.
- Add a data-driven three-stage colony progress tree, hidden in debugging mode and rendered as a non-overlapping checklist at the right playfield border. Construction status frames reserve the checklist area at every supported UI scale. Future stages are added by appending another requirement table/stage definition.
- Stage 1 requires a completed Solar Collector, Battery, Water Driller and Water Storage in one physically connected completed Supply Channel component. Stage 2 requires the first completed Air Lock, an Oxygen Generator and an actual pipe-network path linking the resource network to an inside structure.
- Stage 3 requires a Canteen, completed Crew Quarters with capacity for at least six people, a Landing Pad and every completed indoor/storage volume at >=95% breathable air. Completing all three stages unlocks the milestone READY FOR HUMAN CREW.
- Progress and demand telemetry are derived at runtime; save schema remains 19. Add progression and UI-scale regressions while retaining existing navigation, logistics and construction behavior. Update installers continue to list every changed file and never build.

0.43.13 - three-chamber bidirectional air lock
- Divide the existing air-lock envelope into an exterior isolation cabin, a central pressure chamber and an interior access cabin. Add two physical internal bulkheads and four animated pressure seals, retaining both authored end hatches, size tiers, footprints and corridor interfaces.
- Determine the inside/outside orientation from the completed corridor attachment. Approach the next door to pass either way, or reverse/retreat at any stage. One controller owns all four seals; previous doors must close completely before the next opens. A body in an open aperture prevents closing on the avatar.
- Equalize the central chamber gradually toward the approached side, only with every seal closed. Internal doors open only at matching pressure. The displayed cycle pressure is relative to the colony interior; breathable-air inventory and the existing supply simulation remain separate. No through-path to the surface is opened.
- Match POV collision to the complete internal bulkheads, including aperture width, shoulders and overhead clearance. Block walking around, jumping over and tunneling through a closed partition.
- Show current cabin, center pressure and sealing/equalization state in the POV HUD and selected-air-lock details. Existing air locks gain the internal layout on load; transient cycle state resets with closed seals. Save schema remains 19.
- Add full physical crossings in both directions and center-chamber retreats across rotated small/standard variants; retain single-side connection rules. Update geometric traversal checks to wait for actual pressure cycles. Update installers continue to list every changed file and never build.

0.43.12 - keep one air-lock side free
- Air Locks dock corridors only at their two authored end hatches; the curved hull no longer receives generated side doors. Either end can be selected for the first connection.
- A corridor or Supply Channel blueprint immediately reserves its side. Reject new corridor, utility and walkway attachments on the opposite side in placement previews and before committing. Same-side corridors reuse the existing sealed trunk, and removing the final connection releases the side.
- Keep the unused hatch, stairs, surface/POV access and pressure-door interlock. Existing saved links are preserved; new placements cannot extend an already conflicting two-sided Air Lock. Save schema remains 19, with reservations derived from actual links rather than new persistent flags.
- Add rotated small/standard Air Lock regressions for source/destination rejection, blueprint reservations, utility-first ordering, shared-trunk branching, reservation release and exterior access.

0.43.11 - floating FIFO priorities and storage flow rates
- Default new UI preferences to 100%; preserve saved UI-size choices and the shared resolution transform.
- Move PRIORITIZE BUILD / REMOVE PRIORITY into the selected unfinished object's floating construction frame. Show its PRIO queue position in that frame and keep overhead badges on other promoted objects. Protect the floating button from world-selection input.
- Append manual priorities in first-in-first-solved order. Keep later queued objects from receiving new assignments until the head completes or is removed; prune completed/deleted entries. Allow the head's unfinished prerequisites to progress so the queue cannot block itself. Active build work and loaded deliveries finish safely.
- Automatically prefer unfinished corridors and Supply Channels whose two closed endpoints are already complete, ahead of unfinished structures, including manually queued buildings. Show AUTO LINK on these ready connections.
- Add a water-tank fill bar and signed POWER / WATER rate indicators. Measure actual net battery charging/draining in kW and net tank filling/draining in litres per game day; hover text also reports actual supplied electrical load and water consumption. Capacity, input and charge limits affect the measured values. Paused indicators report PAUSED.
- Save schema 19 retains stable priority IDs and migrates schema-18 newest-first lists into chronological FIFO order. Derived flow telemetry remains transient; older saves remain readable.
- Extend queue, migration, automatic-link, prerequisite, charge/fill/drain and floating-button resolution regressions. Keep installer file summaries and never build from an update installer.

0.43.10 - update file summaries and colony status indicators
- Shell and batch installers print the update version and the complete list of added, modified and deleted workspace files after successful application. Include update-summary.txt in each new package. Repeat applications and rejected updates report zero changed files; neither installer builds.
- Add a battery-charge indicator below the POWER status tile. Use actual completed-battery kWh divided by their combined 60 kWh standard / 30 kWh small capacity; show matching decimal inventory totals instead of placeholder counters.
- Add an AIR status tile and indicator for the combined breathable-air fill of all completed indoor habitats and Storage Domes. Weight each interior by volume, include disconnected interiors, and exclude blueprints, exterior service manifolds and connection volumes. Newly completed interiors with no refreshed supply volume still count using their model envelope.
- Empty colonies show AIR -- and zero fill. Clamp readings to valid bounds; low charge/air uses the existing warning color. Hover text explains battery charge and the volume-weighted air average. Keep the shared resolution/UI transform and accommodate all existing resource tiles.
- Save schema remains 18; this is presentation and installer behavior only.

0.43.9 - recover routes into and out of narrow service bays
- Reproduce the 0-43-7 save's CR-01 stall with 21 kg aboard: the actual supply-channel delivery point is clear, but the start-relative coarse lattice has no free terminal row between the water driller and water storage.
- After two failures of the existing incremental/coarse planners, escalate to a retained full-playfield fallback with a destination-aligned lattice and nominal 1.05 m cells. Successful existing routes keep their previous planner and traffic behavior.
- Find a checked connection from the exact robot position to a grid entry up to twelve cells away, and retain that entry in the extracted route. Smoothing can no longer omit the necessary first bend when leaving a narrow bay.
- Limit the global graph to 40000 cells, retain the expansion budget and check a 4 ms propagation deadline. Defer unfinished searches across updates; reseed changed global geometry lazily instead of resampling all known nodes in a single revision pass. This deadline does not cap the entire game frame or an individual geometry callback.
- Preserve pending global parking searches across idle dispatch slices. Every path still passes exact segment clearance and shared motion-contract checks; no collision radius, physical obstacle or cargo accounting is weakened.
- Save schema stays 18; old saves load directly and planner state remains transient. Add focused narrow-entry/exit, broad detour, map-edge and obstacle-change regressions plus an optional saved-route replay command.

0.43.8 - debug idle-robot movement and construction priorities
- In debug mode select an idle robot, use DEBUG MOVE / PICK DESTINATION, then click the ground. The robot uses the normal collision-checked planner and certified motion contracts, and holds the chosen position after arrival. Reissuing moves the destination; Cancel stops the custom order, and Escape/right-click cancels destination picking.
- Keep constructor/carrier assignment active while a debug move or hold exists. Real work immediately drops the custom destination, including during motion. Busy robots, loaded/reserved cargo, active actions and delivery-exit phases cannot receive debug move orders. Disable debug mode to release all custom moves. Debug destinations are transient and are not restored from saves.
- Add PRIORITIZE BUILD / NORMAL PRIORITY to the shared details header for every unfinished habitat, resource structure/extension, corridor, supply channel and walkway. New promotions go to the front; toggling off restores ordinary ordering. The control remains available outside debug mode.
- Apply priority tiers before carrier FIFO and before builder distance/team scoring. Existing dependencies, supplied-material gates, stage safety and loaded cargo delivery remain authoritative; active build jobs finish normally before reassignment.
- Show cyan PRIO badges above promoted unfinished objects, using the shared resolution/UI transform. Completed objects lose the badge and promotion control.
- Save schema 18 appends the ordered stable placement IDs, preserving priority across loading and vector reindexing. Older schemas remain readable and default to normal priority. New colonies and debug blueprint imports clear the previous colony's priorities.

0.43.7 - reduce idle-colony CPU and graphics submission load
- Cache the four immutable storage packing grids instead of regenerating and sorting a grid for every rendered or navigation-tested inventory box.
- Remove redundant indoor-box obstacles from outdoor rover snapshots. Completed dome envelopes already enclose their entire contents; ground and site packages retain collision checks, and POV picking/collision is unchanged.
- Batch warehouse package faces and outlines, reducing this save's 3D submissions from 1827 to 162 while retaining the same 472386 submitted vertices and individual physical packages.
- Stream ordered OpenGL geometry once per projection/depth pass, orphaning previous GPU backing before upload. Preserve draw order, colors, widths and world/HUD boundaries; stop repeatedly replacing offset zero while previous draws may still use it. Direct3D already streams frame geometry.
- Give completed exterior equipment a 20-litre sealed service manifold, allowing oxygen to traverse equipment ports between supply pipes. Every habitat kind, storage dome and connected corridor remains a finite air volume; filling is gradual and consumes real connected tank water.
- Double solar peak production from 8 to 16 kW standard and from 4 to 8 kW small. Keep nightfall, dispatch, battery capacity and charge limits; use the same peak constant in telemetry.
- Save schema remains 17; v0.43.6 saves load directly. CPU workers already use all permitted processors; avoid manufacturing background work just to fill idle CPUs.

0.43.6 - compact storage gates, POV jump and warning reasons
- Replace angle/size-dependent gate stretching with one rigid 2.0 m transfer chamber (2.2 m external depth, 2.6 m width). Preview, completed mesh, shutters, robot service point and clearance use the same radial anchors at every angle and dome tier.
- Completed storage domes use a conservative circular rover envelope instead of square corners. Keep exact swept-disc collision checks and reserve the fully opened cargo shutters as solid rover geometry. Gates remain separate construction tasks, with up to three per dome.
- Keep the pickup/drop-off point 1.55 m in front of the gate. Reserve the compact staging bay, full shutter travel and a clearance margin against structures, corridors, walkways, pipes, material piles and other cargo approaches.
- Remove unused sealed-door candidate placing pads/reservations and persistent orange cross-filled squares. Retain actual door/cargo clearances, show selected cargo pads as cyan outlines, and show the accepted/rejected approach boundary during gate placement.
- Space triggers a grounded POV jump with lunar gravity, floor landing and body/head clearance against pressure roofs, docking sleeves and door headers. Holding Space does not auto-jump and pressing it in the air does not reset velocity. Add press/release/focus handling on Linux and Windows and update POV help.
- Every habitat and outdoor/resource structure shares warning-reason rendering before specialized panel branches. Explain all active warning bits, including missing connected battery/tank, low power/water/air/stock/food/medical. Wrap multiple reasons and grow the details frame consistently without changing screen scaling.
- Save schema remains 17; existing gate tasks/angles are retained and their compact positions are derived on synchronization/load.

0.43.5 - water-to-oxygen processing and breathable-air telemetry
- Report BREATHABLE AIR percentage and interior volume in habitat, storage-dome and corridor details. Show signed percentage-point change per game day; habitats/corridors also show net oxygen change.
- Oxygen generator details show measured oxygen output and actual connected-tank water intake, plus processing, paused, missing-input and full-network status. Displayed rates reflect actual simulated production/transfer/consumption, including power, water and capacity limits.
- Retain tank-fed electrolysis (1.125 L water per kg O2) and gradual, conservative distribution through completed pipes/corridors. Capture actual tank debit as the production source; no disconnected or global water inventory is used.
- Refresh derived cabin volumes/percentages and supply availability on save load without consuming resources or advancing time. Pause masks rate telemetry; transient measured rates are recomputed rather than persisted. Save schema remains 17.
- Add dedicated electrolysis/air-rate regression and expand resolution/zoom UI coverage from 325 to 400 cases, including filling and depleting interiors and generator details.

0.43.4 - one resolution-independent UI layout
- Fit a 1920 x 1080 reference canvas to the actual viewport, then apply the saved UI-size preference. At the same aspect ratio, 720p/900p/1080p/1440p/4K use identical logical layout, text, icon and control proportions.
- Update viewport/scale before input processing on both platforms and at render boundaries; resolution changes, saved UI preferences and mouse hitboxes use the same transform. UI-size changes take effect on the following frame.
- Shared font metrics and glyph atlas for OpenGL and Direct3D, including missing UI punctuation. Use a common readable caption floor and button typography; fitted captions respect shared padding and panel/cell bounds.
- Draw 2D strokes as logical-width triangle quads, avoiding fixed pixel-width borders and driver wide-line differences. Remove the construction callout border's duplicate scale multiplication.
- Render procedural icons from fixed design grids. Crowded catalogs page/scroll with fixed-size icons, instead of shrinking the artwork independently of the UI-size preference.
- Fit long status/debug/selection/callout text to its own bounds; wrap gameplay help; put contextual selection in a shared top inset; reserve HUD space for construction callouts; use a two-column new-game form at larger UI-size settings.
- Scale world cursor arms/thickness with UI size and resolution, keep the desktop cursor over selection controls, and consume catalog wheel input before it can zoom the world.
- Add UI regressions covering 325 screen/zoom/resolution combinations, aspect ratios, button/selection hitboxes, frame-boundary preference changes, catalog paging/wheel input, text metrics and bounds. Save schema remains 17.

0.43.3 - automatic safety-fault route recovery
- Each fault schedules a fresh route attempt within three simulation seconds. Persistent geometry/bounds faults retry on the same maximum interval; repeated fault detection does not move the deadline.
- Discard cached routes, D* search state, motion proposals, temporary retreats, stationary detours and steering history; plan from the measured position to the latest active scheduled destination.
- Recovery planning runs before the invariant-fault early return and does not wait behind ordinary route-search priority. Searches remain bounded and independent jobs run through the task pool.
- Resume through a new stationary hold and normal certified motion only if current geometry, motion bounds and fleet separation are safe. Idle/work holds do not invent a destination.
- Preserve task/cargo/reservation state while fault recovery runs; ordinary work/traffic logic cannot silently downgrade Fault. Display the retry countdown and attempt count in robot status.
- Save schema remains 17; the fault deadline/search state are transient, as before.

0.43.2 - gate placing pads and connected colony supplies
- Highlight cargo transfer pads beside the gate and crew placing pads beside each authored/generated door, including blueprint previews; reject structure, corridor, walkway and supply-channel overlap with reserved pads.
- Restore continuous cursor-driven habitat and storage corridor attachment with local magnetic snapping to authored doors. Cargo gates support continuous yaw with local snapping to predefined cargo sectors; keep three separately constructed gates per warehouse.
- Extend the cargo transfer sleeve to the existing rover-safe approach envelope and keep the placing pad 1.55 m from its outer gate; retain shared-gate logistics and safety coordination.
- Solar generation feeds local loads and charges connected batteries; night loads discharge them. Water drillers fill connected tanks directly. Both inventories are finite and never shared across disconnected networks.
- Design ratings (standard): solar 8 kW peak, battery 60 kWh/10 kW charge-discharge, tank 2,000 L, driller 500 L/day at 4 kW, electrolyzer 9 kg O2/day at 2.8 kW. Small outdoor equipment uses half capacity/rate; battery charge efficiency is 95%.
- Production rebalance per plant/day: iron 40 kg, copper 4 kg, silicon 4 kg, aluminum 8 kg; plastics 10 kg; edible food 18 kg per bio-dome capacity unit; medical supplies 0.30 kg. These are explicit game design ratings, not validated equipment specifications.
- Crew per day: 0.84 kg O2, 4 L make-up water, 0.85 kg food, 0.03 kg medical reserve. Water comes from the connected tanks. Food and medical use the existing physical package inventory. Indoor production requires at least 95% target air.
- Cabin target: 70 kPa, 293 K, 30% O2 with assumed reserve buffer gas. Compute oxygen from the interior cubic envelope (65% of mesh envelope after floor height), and electrolysis water at 1.125 L/kg O2. This is an estimated usable volume, not mesh-volume integration or a full fluid/composition solver.
- Pressure-driven, simultaneous, mass-conserving oxygen transfer across completed pipes, corridors and their junctions. Link conductance decreases with route length; downstream structures fill later. Unfinished structures/links do not transmit services.
- Display AIR percentage, volume and remaining water to fill per interior; habitat panel includes connected-network water deficit. Display producer rates and actual battery/tank quantities, with slowly pulsing warning triangles and specific missing inputs.
- Save schema 17 persists battery energy, tank water, oxygen in structures/pipes/corridors, and continuous cargo gate yaw. Older saves remain readable; previously fictional colony counters do not seed new physical inventories.
- Oxygen reference: https://www.nasa.gov/reference/environmental-control-and-life-support-systems-eclss/
- Crew oxygen planning reference: https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20200001090/downloads/20200001090.pdf
- Generator capacity reference: https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Human_and_Robotic_Exploration/Node-3_Cupola/Oxygen_Generation_System_Rack

0.40.51 - corner-safe D* smoothing and longer look-ahead
- D* line-of-sight smoothing now preserves soft structure-clearance cost classes; it may not shortcut a safe route back through a higher-cost corner halo.
- Sharp route corners are mandatory waypoints with a 0.20 m acceptance radius; visibility following cannot skip across them.
- Rover speed is capped/braked on approach to sharp route corners so the swept chassis can complete the turn instead of repeatedly rejecting forward proposals.
- Motion prediction horizon doubled from 0.50 s to 1.00 s.
- Planner/proposal refresh reduced from 20 Hz to 10 Hz; the authoritative motion-contract tick remains 100 Hz.

0.40.50 - host-wide multithreaded CPU allocation
- Runtime TaskPool now uses all logical CPUs visible to the process by default (main lane + N-1 persistent helpers); GAME_CPU_LANES can cap it.
- Removed the two-heavy-route-search ceiling; concurrent route repair/search scales up to the available CPU lanes and fleet size.
- World-revision route validation is parallelized per rover before planning.
- Heavy planner jobs are prioritized into the dynamic work queue before cheap proposal refreshes.
- Deterministic reservation arbitration, pose commit and game-world/resource mutation remain serialized.
- Clean builds now default to all visible logical CPUs; GAME_BUILD_JOBS can cap build parallelism.

0.40.29
- Added persistent, type/size-aware unique names for every placed habitat and resource-structure blueprint; names remain unchanged after construction and across save/load.
- Expanded selected-robot telemetry with contextual task/action, named target, navigation/traffic state, blocker details, construction progress, cargo/reservation and worksite material state.

0.40.28
- Construction blueprint HUD: unselected unfinished blueprints now render only a compact construction progress bar.
- Selecting an unfinished habitat, resource structure, corridor, supply channel or walkway restores the detailed step/progress/material frame.
- Passive blueprint status rendering no longer calculates shipped/needed material text unless that blueprint is selected.
- Raised the shared default robot movement multiplier from 150% to 200%; builder and carrier transit speeds, acceleration, braking and turning scale consistently, and loaded saves normalize to the new role defaults.

0.40.27
- Replaced full-destination rover route publication with a 0.5 s receding/rolling navigation horizon.
- Each short horizon is re-planned from the robot's current pose and validates completed/partial structures, staged resources, terrain/slope and predicted robot hull motion before it is committed.
- Added deterministic local collision fallback trajectories (lateral, angled and reverse) when the preferred horizon is blocked; fallback remains bounded to the same <=0.5 s planning window.
- Limited fleet conflict prediction to the same 0.5 s horizon and shortened the WAIT/PASS-to-fallback transition so changing traffic is handled locally instead of triggering long full-map searches.
- Robot inspector/debug route rendering now identifies the rolling horizon / local fallback rather than presenting it as a complete trip route.

0.40.26
- Fixed corridor fan ownership so a branch on a corridor between two structures is optimized from only the nearest entrance; this prevents the second pass from flipping the branch into a self-overlapping U-turn.
- Added a corridor self-overlap safety check and a load-time repair for invalid saved corridor topology.
- Corridor placement now suppresses only true duplicate physical links, allowing triangle links and new links between already-blueprinted corridor runs in the same network component.
- Initial corridor-on-corridor blueprint preview now shows only the junction/adapter fitting until a destination is selected.

0.40.22 - Multi-structure robot deadlock recovery; universal SMALL structure tier; mouse-wheel size-only placement.
0.40.4
- Added PAUSE as a fourth game-speed state alongside NORMAL 1X / QUICK 2X / FAST 4X. Pause uses a 0X simulation multiplier so colony time, robots, production and construction stop while the UI/camera remain responsive; +/- cycles PAUSE <-> NORMAL <-> QUICK <-> FAST and the selected state persists through save/load.

0.40.3
- CR-01 carriers now reserve and deliver the complete remaining bill of materials for active constructions, continuing repeated trips until all outstanding demand is staged or covered by fleet reservations.
- All robot locomotion is 50% faster; acceleration, braking and turn rates scale with the new movement speed to preserve smooth route following.
- Construction progress is now continuous inside every welding/build-up operation; HUD percentages and staged wire-mesh reveal advance throughout each builder action instead of only jumping when a full stage commits.

0.19.1
- Returned to plain release.major.minor versioning.
- Ground resource boxes are selectable and show material, amount and condition in an adjacent status frame.
- Added buildable Storage Dome with 100,000 kg material capacity.
- In debug mode, DEL transfers a selected ground resource box into an available completed Storage Dome.

0.19.0-dev.1
- Switched development builds to SemVer prerelease numbering: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH-dev.N.
- Demolishing habitat/resource structures drops the materials actually consumed by completed construction stages onto the ground.
- Dropped material piles occupy ground and invalidate overlapping structure placement.
- Normal mode now reserves the remaining staged cost of active construction when validating new builds, preventing material overcommit; debug mode still permits negative stock.

