0.18.9 - Placement intersection preview refinement
- Routed build tools show a real one-section blueprint before the first click.
- Walkways prefer valid existing-network intersection snaps; invalid nearby intersections stay red.
- Supply channels can start/end on open terrain and snap to existing channel centerlines for junctions.
- Corridor/walkway/supply source samples use the same validity rules as final placement.
0.18.9 -> 0.18.10 : walkway branching now mirrors corridor branching: terminal snaps extend on the host tangent, mid-run branches leave on the destination-side perpendicular, and exact shared host centerline vertices produce deterministic seamless T/Y junctions without overlap
0.18.10 -> 0.18.11 : cumulative four-material construction economy integration retained/packaged; walkway blueprint routing now uses corridor-style fixed-order curve sampling and deterministic route-side selection to prevent section/frame flipping while moving the cursor
0.19.2 - Storage Dome moved to Habitats/Infrastructure; Small/Mid/Big/Huge tiers; fixed corridor/supply docking ports; dome/network placement snapping.
0.19.2 -> 0.19.3 : physical Storage Dome inventory boxes with adaptive pallet stacking and exact box persistence; dome selection inventory by resource; Water, Food and Medical Supplies added to resource management/status bar/storage accounting.
0.19.3 -> 0.19.4 : corridor and supply-channel construction costs now use routed length, bend count and junction/docking hardware; staged Fe/Cu/Si/plastic requirements are calculated from the actual route geometry and shown in selection details.
0.19.4 -> 0.19.5 : exact demolition recovery and Storage Dome box accounting; debug removal routes consumed construction material into physical stacked dome boxes when capacity exists; dome totals are recalculated from boxes; all habitat/resource/corridor/supply/walkway selection panels show the actually consumed material that will be freed on removal.
0.19.5 -> 0.19.6 : routed demolition recovery now drops exact consumed materials along the deleted corridor/channel/walkway footprint; Storage Dome physical boxes are the authoritative inventory and remain visible/accounted even while debug overdraft is negative; network construction consumes matching physical stored boxes without global-stock clamping.
0.19.6 -> 0.19.7 : testing FPV mode; V enters mouse-selected drop mode, first click spawns a relative-mouse first-person camera with WASD/Shift movement, habitat/storage-door traversal, corridor access and automatic proximity-opening doors.
0.19.6 -> 0.19.7 : test first-person base traversal mode on V; captured mouse-look + WASD/Shift movement, outdoor/habitat/storage/corridor collision zones, automatic proximity sliding doors with first-person shell apertures, corridor/open-end traversal and floor-height tracking across terrain/interior decks/corridors.
0.19.7 -> 0.19.8 : FPV avatar scaled to corridor/door clearance with body-radius-aware corridor traversal; X11 relative mouse capture now follows window focus and releases the pointer immediately when the game loses focus.

0.20.2 - Corridor junction merge geometry rebuilt to carve host apertures and prevent overlapping corridor intersections.
0.20.2 -> 0.20.3 : 10x per-box carry/storage mass; Storage Dome capacity derived from usable interior volume; layer-major physical box stacks with actual package-height accumulation; dome packing constrained to a conservative cylinder fully inside the pressure hull.
0.20.3 -> 0.20.4 : corridor/structure access overhaul; unified structure floors and door thresholds with corridor deck level, removed corridor-colliding exterior stairs, tightened corridor-to-corridor and corridor-to-structure clearance, prevented later structure placement over corridors, and replaced broad FPV door-radius hull escapes with exact doorway/vestibule portal traversal.
0.20.4 -> 0.20.5 : FPV drop now resolves the clicked X/Z to the actual walkable floor height inside habitats, Storage Domes, corridors and corridor air-locks; corridor build snapping accepts any existing corridor segment/link/junction as a branch source; every exposed corridor endpoint now includes a collision-checked two-door pressure air-lock with interlocked FPV door rendering and exterior transition at the outer door.
0.20.5 -> 0.20.6 : corridor connection planner overhaul; reused habitat/storage entrances have no fixed branch limit, shared entrance fans are globally rebalanced, and corridor routing searches multiple smooth non-overlapping lanes around existing structures/corridors while explicit corridor targets remain merged shared junctions.
0.20.6 -> 0.20.7 : existing corridors are branchable at arbitrary centerline points, close structure entrances use compact non-overlapping merged connectors, corridor snap reach is expanded, and FPV spawn/floor tracking follows modeled structure basements, docking sleeves and authored stairs.
0.20.7 -> 0.20.8 : corridor interaction reset to node-first placement; any resolvable structure entrance can always be selected as a source regardless of the temporary sample direction, every visible corridor centerline point is prioritized as a source/destination intersection, terminal end snapping is limited to the actual end cap, and distinct points on the same host corridor can be targeted as separate junction nodes.

0.20.9
- Added click-drag resource-box selection pane when no placement tool is active.
- All ground resource packages projected inside the selection pane are selected together.
- Debug DEL can move a multi-selection of resource boxes into available Storage Domes.
- Corridor structure collision now follows actual projected mesh hull/foundation geometry instead of rectangular placement footprints.
- Added obstacle-search corridor routing with progressively wider A* detours around structures and unrelated corridors.
- Corridor routes remain clipped to the active playfield and preserve explicit corridor junction exceptions.
0.20.9 -> 0.21.0 : persistent named savegames; main-menu SAVE/LOAD workflow with text entry and save discovery; versioned atomic save format restores playfield, camera, colony status/time, seven-resource balances, habitats/resource structures, physical ground/storage boxes, construction progress/cost plans, corridor/supply/walkway topology and meshes, UI/debug/view settings, and display/fullscreen settings.
0.21.0 -> 0.21.1 : savegames are stored in a savegames/ directory beside the game executable, with current-working-directory fallback if the executable path cannot be resolved.
0.21.1 -> 0.21.2 : routing stress hardening across every supported playfield size (16..512); corridor fallback now samples multiple points/trunks, can reuse alternate Storage Dome service doors when a selected face is blocked, uses bounded one-bend detours, merges transversal corridor crossings into shared junction geometry, and avoids redundant all-pairs tubes; supply-channel routing is playfield-aware and reuses already-connected utility networks.
0.21.2 -> 0.21.3 : FPV pressure-volume overhaul; Storage Dome packages now rest on the dome's modeled floor, structure containment uses projected pressure-shell mesh geometry with body-radius clearance, structure floors follow modeled basement slabs and entrance stairs, and the FPV HUD explicitly distinguishes pressurized interiors/corridors/airlocks from exterior vacuum.
0.21.3 -> 0.22.0 : corridor placement interaction update; corridor branch previews are rendered at a reduced blueprint scale, explicit corridor-start branches can place valid end points again by fixing long-host-segment junction clearance, and runtime/menu/CLI semantic version reporting is unified through VERSION.
0.22.0 -> 0.22.1 : FPV traversal hardening; bidirectional mesh-level structure-shell containment, doorway-only entry/exit, complete generated docking-vestibule navigation, corridor-to-corridor pressure-volume continuity, diagonal doorway movement, and preserved modeled basement/stair floor tracking.
0.22.1 -> 0.22.2 : structural access hardening; exposed corridor ends are sealed bulkheads, ordinary structures and Storage Domes expose only completed pressure-docked entrances, POV placement is exterior-only, and the dedicated connected Air Lock is the sole exterior gateway into the pressurized corridor/structure network.
0.22.2 -> 0.23.0 : modular habitat/corridor architecture; all ten habitats split into outer and inner builders under the shared StructureModule access contract; explicit per-structure walkable deck heights drive POV and corridor docking stairs; mixed shell/glazing/foundation containment fixes Bio Dome/Cantine interior classification; corridor outer pressure shell and inner walk deck are instantiated through CorridorModule.
0.23.0 -> 0.30.0 : baseline release; filled/detail-enhanced rendering across every habitat, corridor, resource structure, supply channel and walkway; shared future-hardware detail passes; reinforced airtight corridor docking with redundant seals and hard dead-end bulkheads; Air-Lock-only exterior pressure-network access; realistic smoothed POV acceleration/stair motion; canonical astronaut scale reduced to 80%; clean build reports final executable size in KB/bytes.
0.30.0 -> 0.30.1 : corridor intersection manifold refinement; automatic crossing hubs are consolidated by proximity and incoming direction into one faceted airtight pressure manifold with D-profile corridor mouths, eliminating circular/cylindrical overlap geometry.
0.30.1 -> 0.31.0 : autonomous BR-02 construction logistics; new games start with one compact stair/corridor-capable builder robot beside a calculated starter pallet reserve; timer-only construction is disabled and every habitat/resource/corridor/supply/walkway stage now advances only after BR-02 physically collects, transports and delivers that stage's material bundle; mature power/extraction/processing/water/food/medical chains replenish physical stock at sustainable rates; robot/logistics state persists in save schema 2 with schema-1 load compatibility; faceted consolidated corridor intersection manifolds from 0.30.1 remain the only automatic crossing geometry (no circular/cylindrical overlap hubs).
0.31.0 -> 0.31.1 : New Game BR-02 shipment selector (1-6, default 1); selected robots spawn in six marked depot-side bays, autonomously claim different construction targets for parallel work, share physical resource sources, maintain fleet clearance while moving, and are persisted in save schema 3; schema-2 saves load as a one-robot fleet.
0.31.1 -> 0.31.2 : BR-02 collision/animation pass; robot navigation now uses a chassis-sized pressure-shell envelope, avoids incomplete structure footprints, physical ground pallets, Storage Dome cargo boxes and fleet mates, and uses a wider obstacle-steering fan; pickup, put-down, material application, welding and build-up are explicit timed construction actions with animated manipulator/tool effects, and each construction stage commits only after the full visible work sequence completes.
0.31.3 - Persistent construction ghost meshes; smoother BR-02 steering/arrival; moving work-perimeter construction; refined pickup/place/apply/weld/build animations; flat update-package layout.
0.31.3 -> 0.31.4 : construction-aware robot navigation now collides only with actually revealed/erected mesh so robots can cross unbuilt blueprint volume; added CR-01 dedicated carrier logistics with storage-first supply routing and automatic consolidation of loose resources into completed Storage Domes; BR-02 constructors prioritize on-site assembly and self-haul only when carrier support is unavailable; added NORMAL 1X / QUICK 2X / FAST 4X simulation controls in the bottom bar with save persistence.
0.31.4 -> 0.31.5 : robot anti-entrapment construction gating; each stage now performs a fleet-wide future-mesh safety check and evacuates robots from wall-producing habitat/resource footprints or newly revealed network geometry before committing the step; ordinary navigation still collides only with already constructed mesh. Update archives return to one versioned top-level game-update-vX.Y.Z directory.
0.31.5 -> 0.31.6 : independent New Game selectors for 1-6 BR-02 builders and 1-6 CR-01 carriers; carrier logistics remains dedicated to resource-source/depot-to-site hauling while builders self-haul when a starved site has no effective incoming material; all robot travel now uses precomputed collision-aware shortest paths with line-of-sight smoothing and spatial route reservations, preventing fleet routes from intersecting and serializing traffic through narrow passages.
0.31.6 -> 0.31.7 : cooperative construction crews; multiple BR-02 builders and CR-01 carriers may work the same construction target through one shared site buffer/coordinator, carrier deliveries are capacity-reserved to prevent duplicate hauling, builder work is distributed around the target and accelerates moderately with crew size, and CR-01 transit speed is normalized to exactly 110% of BR-02 speed (2.42 m/s vs 2.20 m/s), including older saves.
0.31.7 -> 0.31.8 : CR-01 carrier logistics rebuilt around an explicit per-site required/delivered/reserved/unassigned material ledger; every transport trip reserves an exact material mass before travel, builders only self-haul unreserved shortages, ground-pallet service points and starter-depot spacing guarantee physical pickup access, and homogeneous material trips eliminate multi-stack pickup deadlocks. Every BR-02/CR-01 is individually world-selectable with live status, condition, cargo, route, target and site-ledger inspection. Save schema 5 persists condition/reservations with backward compatibility.
0.31.9 - Persistent construction status frames for every incomplete build object, with remaining resource demand and progress bars.
0.31.9 -> 0.31.10 : placement rotation is N/M-only (mouse wheel remains size selection for scalable objects or camera zoom otherwise); starter resource stacks are aligned to the marked stash on every playfield size with migration of the legacy absolute-Z row; construction status frames show still-needed and shipped/delivered-or-in-transit resources plus the persistent progress bar; robot locomotion adds blocked-route hysteresis and independent visual pose smoothing; logistics reservations expire when transport cannot progress so carrier stalls cannot lock a site, and BR-02 fallback hauling starts promptly for any unassigned shortage.
0.31.10 -> 0.31.11 : construction-site resource deliveries remain visible as staged material boxes until consumed; robot routes are independent and no longer hard-reserved by another robot, with soft path separation and short-horizon reciprocal collision avoidance to prevent fleet deadlocks/stuttering; construction progress frames explicitly use the global UI-scale transform.
0.31.11 -> 0.31.12 : construction rendering is blueprint-only for every incomplete habitat/resource structure/corridor/supply channel/walkway; no filled/material surfaces are emitted before completion, unbuilt geometry is subdued blue, and erected geometry uses brighter substantially thicker blue wireframe lines.
0.31.12 -> 0.31.13 : robot-runtime stabilization; game-speed-independent critically damped robot rendering, stable per-task pickup/delivery/build stations, coarse-grid A* final stand-off connectors and non-orbiting waypoint handling, persistent per-robot recovery service slots, footprint-aware cooperative builder crew limits, local watchdog recovery without fleet-wide waiting, and corrected construction anti-entrapment sequencing that evaluates only newly revealed mesh and evacuates robots to wider exterior safety points before stage commit. Delivered site-buffer resources remain visible as staged boxes and individual robot selection continues to expose task/status, cargo and condition.
0.31.13 -> 0.31.14 : coordinated pre-motion robot route reservations with visible ground routes and deterministic waiting/replanning; builders use fixed construction service stations instead of orbiting/spinning; construction visuals restored as full blue target meshes with progressively replaced yellow installed geometry and bold green completed meshes for habitats, facilities, corridors, supply channels and walkways; construction frames show explicit stage progress.

0.31.14 -> 0.31.15 : hard rendered-hull robot separation with collision-tested turning and post-step overlap repair; co-directional shared routes can operate as spaced convoy lanes while crossing/opposing routes retain right-of-way reservations; CR-01 carriers pre-stage the full remaining construction material backlog, immediately clear completed trip state, and continue accepting follow-up transport work instead of stalling after the first delivery.
0.31.15 -> 0.31.16 : robot autonomy/navigation rewrite; removed reactive steering-fan/retreat motion and replaced it with precomputed spatial A* plus time-expanded cooperative reservation slots inspired by Cooperative A*/SIPP, including explicit planned waits for crossing traffic, swept hull-conflict checks, deterministic fixed-slot execution, and hard no-overlap runtime guards; pickup/construction/Storage Dome service stations now choose hull-safe fleet-separated positions so carriers are never assigned physically impossible destinations; route inspector/ground visualization exposes cooperative travel/hold slots.
0.31.16 -> 0.31.17 : continuous multi-robot traffic overhaul; removed 0.48-second cooperative stop/go slots and render-spring catch-up, replacing them with Theta*-style any-angle route planning, rounded/densified waypoints, full-route fleet trajectory preflight, traffic-weighted alternate lanes, PIBT-inspired dynamic right-of-way aging, continuous reciprocal speed governing and deterministic replan-on-yield without reactive steering/spinning; robot simulation substeps increased to 90 Hz with no dropped FAST-mode remainder; all construction objects are mesh-only with no structure surfaces/textures, retaining blue blueprint / yellow installed / bold green completed wireframes.
0.31.17 -> 0.31.18 : construction-placement and fleet-scheduling stabilization; replaced full-map multi-pass route rasterization with cached corridor-local A* that widens only for required detours, split route-plan budgets between constructors and carriers, rebuilt the starter depot into hull-safe parking plus deterministic material entry/pickup/exit lanes, serialized only same-source pickup traffic, returned idle carriers to clear standby bays, made normal logistics carrier-first with delayed coordinator-only builder fallback, fixed operation stations for each trip, and kept loaded logistics reservations authoritative while blocked. QA covers live second-structure placement with 6 builders + 6 carriers and placement scaling across 16/64/128/256/512 playfields.
0.31.18 -> 0.31.19 : concurrent logistics-lane refinement; BR-02 work stations now follow the actual rotated structure perimeter with ~0.20 m front tool clearance, construction and Storage Dome service positions are hull-reserved before travel, starter-depot same-material trips use a stable staged queue with one-way outer-gate -> lane -> pallet flow and a clear loaded-rover exit, source reservations may queue without double-counting physical stock, fleet prediction integrates actual speed/acceleration rather than assuming instant cruise, carrier site selection balances concurrent construction demand, and any active builder may claim the single stage commit so a delayed original site owner cannot freeze construction. QA places two structures simultaneously with 6 builders + 6 carriers and verifies parallel deliveries/building with zero hull collisions.
0.31.19 -> 0.31.20 : construction logistics handoff overhaul; every site now uses one deterministic visible material drop zone, CR-01s queue/stage/exit around that single apron, BR-02 fallback hauling covers only uncovered stage demand and uses the same drop queue, builders physically collect the current stage bundle from the drop before assembly, every build step is worked from all four structure sides via planned perimeter/corner waypoints, future construction geometry is reserved before segments appear so robots cannot occupy the next-step volume, and carrier depot ownership/queue ageing is stabilized to prevent lane deadlocks during ongoing construction.
0.31.20 -> 0.40.0 : baseline architecture refactor; the game build is partitioned into explicit CMake implementation/context clusters (app, interaction, rendering, UI, construction, robots, pathfinding, simulation, world core, habitats, resources, networks), with a compact cluster map and next-chat baseline context. Robot geometry search is extracted from the monolithic autonomy system into `game_cluster_pathfinding` behind floor/occupancy/segment/traffic callbacks. Route resolution adds a hard 2.0 s watchdog: repeated local replans do not reset it, and after the timeout the rover discards local traffic assumptions and performs a full-width world-geometry route search while runtime hull collision enforcement remains active.

0.40.0 -> 0.40.1 : fixed construction-route deadlocks reproduced from 001-construction-stuck.gmsave; future-segment collision tests now reject degenerate X/Z triangle projections robustly, preventing distant vertical walls from becoming invisible map-wide blockers. Route clearing no longer cancels the 2 s full-resolution escalation through cleared stop-radius metadata. Starter-pallet reservations gain a final lease break if mandatory recovery still cannot move the front rover, and nose-to-nose fleet deadlocks use a deterministic hull-safe pull-aside waypoint for the lower-priority rover before both robots resume their original tasks.
0.40.1 -> 0.40.2 : fixed the no-building.gmsave construction loop and non-holonomic robot sliding; idle CR-01s now vacate active construction approaches into deterministic standby bays and active construction traffic has right-of-way over idle parking traffic. Route execution aligns the chassis to the actual next displacement before translation, allows collision-safe in-place rotation inside soft traffic clearance, and limits emergency separation to longitudinal forward/reverse motion. Starter-depot loaded egress now uses per-load hysteresis so a rover cannot oscillate across or stop just short of the exit threshold. Replay of no-building.gmsave advances the previously stuck structure from step 4 through step 9 with zero audited lateral-motion frames.

v0.40.5
- Resource packages are now first-class navigation obstacles using the full rendered robot hull envelope instead of the smaller legacy body radius.
- Ground resource piles, Storage Dome boxes and visible construction-site staging boxes are avoided by route planning and continuous motion.
- Construction-site packages were moved into a protected offset staging rack beside the shared drop point so builders/carriers can service the drop without driving through the visible crates.
- Ground-pile pickup service distance now matches the full hull clearance used by pathfinding.
- Global builder work speed increased by 50% for welding/build-up operations; stage counts and material costs are unchanged.

0.40.6
- Fixed a severe robot/pathfinding CPU regression reproduced with slowdown.gmsave.
- Dormant step-0 blueprints are no longer reserved as imminent construction geometry until a constructor actually claims them.
- Removed per-path-candidate construction mesh allocations; collision checks now scan only the revealed triangle ranges in-place.
- Added path-based broad-phase rejection for corridor, utility and walkway construction geometry before detailed triangle collision checks.
- Precomputes loose/staged/storage resource package obstacle poses once per fleet simulation slice instead of rebuilding up to 36 site packages for every A* cell and route sample.
- Keeps physical resource avoidance and future-segment safety while preventing repeated 250-300 ms replanning spikes.

0.40.7
- Reworked robot planning performance around the 0-40-6.gmsave regression.
- Route-search budgets are now reset once per rendered update and shared by all 90 Hz motion substeps.
- Failed full-resolution searches honor a backoff and escalation is edge-triggered instead of retriggering every tick.
- Normal A* widening is distributed across later planning opportunities instead of running three searches in one slice.
- Added cheaper neighbour-edge validation while retaining strict final route and execution collision checks.
- Full recovery searches use a coarser bounded grid to eliminate main-thread frame spikes.
- Builders waiting for materials choose the perimeter sector farthest from the construction drop, keeping CR-01 delivery lanes physically solvable.
- Construction-drop package rows are moved farther from the interaction point so resource collision avoidance does not invalidate the handoff position.

0.40.7 -> 0.40.8 : fixed the 0-40-7.gmsave construction/logistics deadlock and added bounded multicore route sampling. Construction-target robots no longer treat their own target's future geometry as a hard service-path blocker; construction package racks are oriented relative to the target so crates do not cross the carrier exit lane; stale carrier reservations stop suppressing BR-02 self-haul after the 2 s unresolved-path threshold; uncovered site demand triggers near-immediate builder fallback while healthy carrier reservations remain authoritative. Broad/full route searches precompute independent occupancy/traffic grid cells across up to half of the system's logical CPUs while routine short replans remain single-threaded to avoid thread-launch jitter.
v0.40.9
- Added space-time collision avoidance inside path calculation: A*/Theta* rejects route edges whose estimated traversal overlaps another robot's predicted hull.
- Kept continuous execution-time hull checks/speed negotiation as a safety layer after preplanned avoidance.
- Reduced builder construction travel from four perimeter work positions to two opposite positions per stage; the pair rotates between stages and forced corner laps were removed.


0.40.9 -> 0.40.10 : fixed the 0-40-9.gmsave construction deadlock by enforcing dependency-ordered corridor construction: corridors now wait for attached habitats/resources and host corridors to finish, and legacy in-progress assignments are suspended without losing staged/cargo materials. Partially installed structure geometry is now a hard rover obstacle as soon as it becomes visually present, eliminating routes through already-built meshes. Construction sequences are compacted from 10 to 6 stages for habitats/resources, 5 to 3 for corridors, and 3 to 2 for supply channels/walkways; older saves are migrated while preserving consumed-versus-remaining material totals.

0.40.10 -> 0.40.11 : fixed the 0-40-10.gmsave same-pallet logistics deadlock. CR-01 now preempts an empty-cargo BR-02 fallback reservation for the same construction site, BR-02 does not self-haul while an idle/active carrier can service that site, and equal-age starter-depot lane ties prefer the carrier over the constructor. Existing saves with both roles already fetching the same site are reconciled at runtime without losing cargo or delivered materials. Delivered construction boxes are now rendered/staged inside habitat/resource footprints and directly on linear blueprints while the physical rover handoff remains outside the future wall envelope, preventing a delivery vehicle from being sealed into new geometry.
0.40.11 -> 0.40.12 : moved delivered construction inventory from BR-02 ownership to each placed blueprint. CR-01 now keeps supplying every unfinished placed habitat/resource blueprint even when no constructor is assigned, and services dependency-ready corridor/utility/walkway blueprints afterward. Migrates schema-5 saves by transferring constructor site buffers onto their targets. Removed the per-stage BR-02 trip to the external drop apron; staged materials are consumed from the blueprint inventory while BR-02 proceeds directly to safe construction work positions, eliminating the 0-40-11 WAITING MATERIALS stall with a fully supplied site. Save schema is now 6.
0.40.12 -> 0.40.13 : construction mesh progress now starts during the first build iteration for habitats/resources. Fixed BR-02 stage-transition stalls by moving normal work bays outside the next-stage safety envelope, latching evacuation destinations so they cannot hop/replan every simulation slice, and making the hard evacuation fallback reachable after one alternate route attempt. Reduced construction-path CPU load by replacing redundant partial-structure triangle collision scans with the already-authoritative footprint exclusion and by computing mesh-instance sin/cos once per transformed vertex batch instead of once per vertex.
0.40.13 -> 0.40.14 : fixed the 0-40-13.gmsave construction stall and made first-iteration construction visually meaningful. Constructors latch stable work/evacuation positions, partial-structure boundary exits are deterministic, idle carriers no longer spend CPU on cosmetic standby A*, and linear-project work/drop lanes avoid blueprint material piles. Habitat/resource construction reveals a meaningful foundation fraction from the first non-zero build iteration; corridor/utility/walkway mesh reveal begins with fractional stage progress.
0.40.14 -> 0.40.15 : fixed the 0-40-14.gmsave rover-body/structure collision deadlock. Structure/navigation safety now accounts for the complete rendered BR-02/CR-01 chassis envelope instead of only the legacy center radius; a constructor whose hull becomes overlapped by newly revealed geometry performs a monotonic one-way body escape before path planning; completed-structure movement checks sample the enlarged hull; future-stage evacuation uses the full chassis radius with an independent hard-recovery clock; and corridor/utility/walkway work bays are kept outside the full-body next-stage envelope. Construction targets are limited to one active BR-02 each so additional constructors remain available for separate blueprints instead of creating perimeter body conflicts.
0.40.15 -> 0.40.16 : fixed the 0-40-15.gmsave third-site logistics deadlock. Construction delivery aprons are now selected from full-chassis-safe perimeter service points and remain reachable near playfield edges; the mid Storage Dome in the regression save no longer targets an impossible CR-01 center position at z=-31.45. BR-02 is now construction-only: constructors never self-haul and are not assigned or moved toward a blueprint until the complete remaining construction resource plan is physically present in that blueprint's site buffer. Legacy under-supplied/in-flight builder assignments are released so CR-01 can finish shipping without builder/carrier crowding. Starter-depot egress is no longer east-only: loaded carriers select a full-hull-safe exit when a completed structure blocks the normal gate. Completed habitats/resources remain permanent full-body rover obstacles, legacy saves relocate only rovers already overlapping such a footprint, and structures with a planned unfinished corridor are worked from that connector side throughout construction so BR-02 is not sealed onto the wrong side before the next fully-stocked target becomes eligible. Linear construction work bays are full-hull-safe and latched for each build stage so the selected side cannot flip as BR-02 crosses a corridor centerline.
0.40.16 -> 0.40.17 : fixed the 0-40-16.gmsave circular starter-depot deadlock. A BR-02 waiting for a blueprint to become fully stocked can no longer remain parked in the CR-01 pallet ingress/egress spine; idle constructors inside that logistics-reserved zone make only a short, full-hull-checked move to a validated standby bay and still never acquire or move toward a construction target until its complete remaining resource plan is physically delivered. This keeps the strict carrier-first construction rule while guaranteeing the carrier can reach the final pallet that unlocks the builder.
0.40.17 -> 0.40.18 : reworked rover locomotion from stop-turn-segment driving to continuous curved steering. Spatial routes now use wider collision-validated quadratic corner fillets with denser sampling; the route follower looks ahead along the curve, reduces speed according to curvature/heading error, and turns the chassis while translating through ordinary bends instead of enforcing the old 6-degree stop-and-align gate. Short construction/service-apron maneuvers also integrate a collision-checked steering arc. Near-U-turns and emergency traffic escapes may still align in place when required for full-hull safety.
0.40.18 -> 0.40.19 : restored the proven 0.40.17 rover movement executor after the 0.40.18 curved-steering regression caused robots to stall. Kept the existing full-hull collision/deadlock fixes, but reverted the wider arc fillets, denser 0.22 m route sampling and continuous look-ahead steering changes. Reworked synchronous pathfinding performance for construction/logistics: impossible goals are rejected before A*, grid terrain/occupancy samples are cached lazily, route-distance prediction uses binary search, rover path validation no longer invokes the astronaut pressure-volume solver, staged resource obstacles are cached once per fleet slice, pending habitat/resource mesh collision uses a cheap footprint broad phase before triangle tests, and completed corridor footprints use a cheap conservative nav mask. This removes the large main-thread planning spikes seen when corridor construction/logistics begins while preserving the 0.40.17 driving behavior.
0.40.19 -> 0.40.20 : made dependency-ready corridors/network blueprints first-class construction targets and added a mandatory pre-build blueprint evacuation gate. Before BR-02 may acquire or start any untouched habitat, resource structure, corridor, supply channel or walkway, every rover hull must be outside the complete planned footprint/strip; blockers are moved to a validated exterior point with exclusive short-range safety motion. Builder assignment and the first Building transition both re-check the invariant. Dependency-ready corridors are hard-prioritized for CR-01 logistics and fully supplied ready corridors for BR-02 construction; carriers now rescan the global queue after every unload instead of immediately reserving another trip for the same unrelated structure. Linear blueprints use a perpendicular full-hull-safe delivery apron outside the future tube while resource boxes remain visually staged inside the blueprint, preventing corridor deliveries from targeting narrow/unreachable endpoint throats.

0.40.20 -> 0.40.21 : fixed corridor construction work-bay deadlock: linear work bays now require conservative full-body fleet clearance, and persistent watchdog service-slot retries alternate side/along-path selection instead of recomputing the same blocked bay.

0.40.23 - Buried supply channels below rover plane; rover navigation ignores built/partial channel geometry; schema-8 migration lowers legacy channels.

0.40.24: dedicated stationary robot spawn apron; smallest-size placement defaults; flat visible rover-crossable supply channels with schema-9 migration.

0.40.24 -> 0.40.25 : rewrote rover navigation so static A*/Theta* pathfinding no longer treats temporary fleet traffic as world geometry. Added pair-stable right-of-way with explicit WAIT/PASS state, full-hull collision guarding, breadcrumb backout through one-rover-wide sections, first-available passing-pocket selection, and a single-rover detour fallback only after cooperative yielding fails. Tightened the static navigation grid to preserve narrow valid passages, moved the stationary spawn apron beside the starter resource stash, and exposed WAIT/PASS/DETOUR in robot route status.

0.40.31 -> 0.40.32 : added per-rover local fleet awareness and 0.5-second prediction against nearby robots' certified motion/braking envelopes; coarse replans treat current rover positions as temporary obstacles; idle/working robots are never moved for collision avoidance, and only actively navigating robots may receive deadlock retreat goals.
0.40.32 -> 0.40.33 : added New Game DEBUG START TEMPLATE option; imports the supplied 64x64 construction layout as fresh blueprints only, preserves the newly configured fleet/starter stash, and starts paused.
0.40.42 -> 0.40.43 : require direct hardware GLX/OpenGL and reject software rasterizers; expose GPU renderer in the debug frame; add a persistent half-CPU worker pool and parallelize independent rover route/proposal preparation while keeping safety arbitration/world commits serialized; default builds use half of visible logical CPUs.

0.40.44
- Fixed BR-02 long-detour routing around structure-connected corridor barriers by widening the bounded fallback search and adding wider cheap doglegs.
- Construction is stage-fed: BR-02 starts once the next stage is delivered; CR-01 stages materials for every blueprint independently of build dependency order.
- Corridor, supply-channel and walkway blueprints can receive CR-01 deliveries before their endpoints are build-ready.
- Optimized OpenGL dynamic VBO uploads and bitmap text batching to remove thousands of tiny per-frame GPU submissions.
- Stable coarse routes are now retained while a rover is making progress; full replans happen only after real world/goal changes or blocking recovery instead of every small steering deviation.
- Carrier site selection now seeds the least-supplied next stage across structures, corridors, supply channels and walkways before repeatedly topping up one convenient target.

0.40.45
- Fixed CR-01 pickup transaction restart on dependency-blocked network blueprints.
- Construction dependencies now gate BR-02 work only; CR-01 may keep staging materials for all blueprints.
- Staged corridor/supply/walkway resources are preserved while endpoint dependencies are incomplete.

## v0.40.46
- CR-01 carrier logistics now follows one global FIFO blueprint queue in exact placement order.
- Each blueprint is fully supplied for its entire remaining construction plan before carriers advance to the next one.
- Placement order is persisted across habitat, resource-structure, corridor, supply-channel, and walkway blueprints (save schema 13).
0.40.47 - BR-02 idle parking: constructors release construction bays while waiting for carrier materials and return to their dedicated stash-side parking bay when no executable construction task exists.
0.40.48 - structure-detour routing and bounded fleet replanning
- Coarse A* now has a longitudinal escape band, so a rover can back away from a blocking structure before routing around it instead of being trapped inside the old start-to-goal search strip.
- Wide fallback routing reaches farther around large structures while retaining a fine physical-clearance fallback for narrow passages.
- Construction/world revisions validate each rover's existing simplified route first; unrelated routes are retained instead of forcing a fleet-wide A* rebuild.
- Full coarse-route searches are budgeted across rendered frames and prioritized by task priority/blocking age, preventing simultaneous multi-robot replans from creating large input/render stalls.
- Repeated no-route searches use deterministic exponential retry backoff, and ground-resource inventory changes no longer invalidate navigation unless the obstacle geometry actually changes.

0.40.49 - incremental D* Lite navigation and clearance cost field
- Normal global rover routes now retain per-robot D* Lite g/rhs state and repair changed static geometry incrementally instead of rebuilding the entire route search.
- D* propagation is bounded and resumable across rendered frames; a budget-limited search holds safely and continues later rather than triggering an immediate expensive A* retry.
- Global static routing excludes other robots. Robot-vs-robot conflicts remain under local prediction, passage reservations, stationary detours, waiting and certified retreat.
- Structures, corridors and ground resources use a soft graded clearance/inflation cost so open space is preferred while narrow physically valid passages remain available. Terrain grade is a secondary route cost.
- A* remains as the fleet-aware/recovery fallback and the existing 10 ms motion-contract collision authority is unchanged.


0.41.6: Fixed shared storage loading gates, reserved cargo aprons, sealed sliding corridor doors with swept collision and Air Lock interlocks, schema 14 gate-face persistence.

0.41.7: Buildable outdoor four-bay robot workshop, automatic ramp gate, stable idle parking/task dispatch, docked condition recovery, protected reachable approach, and planner intake for stationary holds.

0.41.8: Up to two resource-built storage gate upgrades on planned/completed domes, protected loading aprons, independent cargo stations, persistent parent links, cascade removal, and blue/yellow/green meshes for all pressure doors and loading gates.

0.42.0: Unified Linux/Windows source workspace; Win32 + Direct3D 12/DXGI backend; matched shell/batch development scripts; isolated platform build trees; cross-platform executable paths and CPU topology; preserves v0.41.8 gameplay/save baseline.

0.42.1: Linux-focused gameplay update: Storage Dome placement is now two-stage (shell/size first, then a cargo gate in a free inter-door sector); Robot Workshops require a completed Supply Channel to become operational; workshop hall reduced to an 11 x 10 m footprint while retaining four service bays, gate/ramp access, and a protected maneuvering apron.

0.42.2: Robot path-negotiation performance pass (no gameplay/feature changes). Rover clearance queries use a per-tick structure snapshot (precomputed rotations, pending-target bounds, cached wall triangles per construction stage); one exact swept test replaces per-0.18 m structure samples; robot-vs-robot contract arbitration skips non-neighbouring contracts; pull-out/detour candidate A* searches run across the worker pool and pick the same winner; light planner batches no longer wake helper threads; TaskPool::parallel_for dispatch is allocation-light; the concurrent heavy route-search cap is fixed at two so fleet behaviour no longer depends on core count (6+ lane hosts livelocked the construction fleet).
0.42.3: Robot Workshop maneuvering clearance is now placement-only. The protected approach/apron remains enforced while placing workshop and neighboring blueprints, but unfinished-workshop robot construction/navigation processing now uses the same normal structure footprint clearance as other resource structures. Physical workshop walls, ramp, gate collision, parking access, and supply-channel operation are unchanged.

0.43.0: Connected pipe-network utilities and seamless Storage Dome service attachment.
- Completed Supply Channels and completed pressure Corridors now form one physical utility topology; corridors carry embedded water and power service lines through bends and junctions.
- Power and water availability are evaluated independently per connected network component. Separate networks cannot borrow generation or water treatment from each other, and unfinished links do not carry service.
- Resource/inside-base production dependencies are component-local, preventing ore/water/industrial feedstock from implicitly teleporting between disconnected utility networks.
- Robot Workshops still require a completed direct Supply Channel and now additionally require actual power to be available on that connected network before parking/maintenance becomes operational.
- Supply Channels can branch directly onto completed corridor sides, and corridor removal safely detaches any utility endpoints that referenced it.
- Storage Dome corridor and Supply Channel attachment is cursor-driven around the free hull surface. Cargo-gate sectors remain protected, but ordinary connections are no longer quantized to four 90-degree/cardinal faces; routed supply channels use free-angle smoothed curves.
- Linux build summary again prints the final executable size in KB and bytes.
- Added a utility-network regression covering component isolation, corridor-carried utilities, unfinished-corridor isolation, and free-angle Storage Dome corridor/supply attachment.

0.43.1: Optional independent Storage Dome cargo-gate extensions.
- Storage Dome placement now creates only the dome blueprint; the mandatory second placement step has been removed.
- A Storage Dome blueprint or completed dome can receive one to three optional Storage Gate extensions, added one at a time from the structure action.
- Every Storage Gate is its own construction task with its own material plan, placement sequence, construction progress, rendering state, and robot work target.
- Gate placement validates and reserves the complete loading apron against map bounds, structures, workshop placement clearance, corridors, walkways, Supply Channels, and resource piles before accepting the extension blueprint.
- Gate-less dome hulls remain fully available for normal seamless/free-angle corridor and Supply Channel attachment; only explicitly placed gate sectors are reserved.
- Existing saves with the former integrated primary cargo gate migrate it into an equivalent child Storage Gate task without changing the save schema.

0.43.16 - adaptive narrow-passage clearance fallback
- Fixed a builder deadlock reproduced by 0-43-14.gmsave: the route was physically valid, but v0.43.15 searched the wide detour only on a coarse 1.70 m grid and searched physical-only clearance only inside a narrower corridor. The required narrow exit vanished on the coarse grid while the narrow fine search could not see the later wide detour.
- Added a precision-passage fallback that reduces only the discretionary structure halo to 0.00 m extra clearance while retaining the full circumscribed rover hull and final motion-contract collision authority.
- Only after the existing direct/dogleg, wide/coarse and fine/local tiers fail does routing run the wide 0.80 m physical-only precision search. Normal construction/service routes therefore keep their established route choices and CPU profile.


0.43.17 - on-demand habitat entrances and rover battery energy
- Remove predefined crew entrances/stair metadata from every habitat template. Corridor connections create generated pressure hatches at the selected hull point; saved legacy authored doors migrate in place as generated physical hatches.
- Keep the Air Lock one-side rule without authored doors: only longitudinal end zones are valid for the base connection, chamber partitions derive from hull dimensions, and the exterior hatch/stairs are synthesized opposite the first completed base connection.
- Add persistent 48 kWh BR-02 / 76 kWh CR-01 traction batteries. Travel, auxiliary electronics and active work consume charge; depleted packs cannot move or advance work. Robot details show kWh, %, draw, charging and low/empty state.
- Powered Robot Workshop bays recharge docked robots at 8 kW input / 92% pack efficiency as a real component-local electrical load. Low-charge idle/unloaded robots return for service and stay until substantially recharged.
- Save schema 21 persists rover battery capacity/charge; older saves receive full role-sized batteries.
- Calibrate the uninterrupted Stage-1/Stage-2 path with one builder and one carrier: no recharge occurs, Stage 2 completes with 15.7% / 13.9% charge remaining, exceeding the requested 10% reserve. Full optimized CTest passes 24/24 groups.

0.43.18 - permanent perimeter rover lane and debug render-layout switch
- Reserves a 2.25 m circulation band inside every playfield edge. New habitat/resource footprints, Robot Workshop approach zones, Storage Dome cargo aprons and generated corridor gate pads may not consume this band, preserving a rover bypass around edge-side construction.
- Existing saves remain loadable without relocating structures; the perimeter rule applies to new placement and newly generated access/service areas.
- Draws the inner edge of the reserved lane as a subtle world-space guide.
- Adds a MESH/TEXTURED layout selector directly to the debug frame. The current renderer has no texture/material asset pipeline, so TEXTURED intentionally falls back to the same procedural mesh rendering until such a backend exists; the game-side mode/state is ready for that future dispatch.

0.43.19 - straight short corridors and continuous 360-degree structure attachments
- Close unobstructed structure-to-structure corridor runs now use one straight centerline segment instead of a tangent-driven cubic/S-curve. Vestibules still handle the local hull/door orientation; obstacles can still force a routed bend.
- Generated habitat corridor anchors follow the exact cursor polar ray around the pressure hull. Removes the four-cardinal-face/seven-slot fallback that caused visible attachment jumps.
- When the exact angle is unavailable, searches nearest free angles in 1-degree increments; existing connections/gates remain real blockers.
- Storage Dome crew-corridor attachment no longer snaps near cardinal positions; cargo-gate sectors remain reserved.
- Optimized release regression: 24/24 registered CTest groups passed.
0.43.20 - shorter initial structure-attached corridor blueprint
- Reduced the pre-click structure corridor sample from 2.75 m to 1.35 m.
- Reduced the fallback start marker from 1.55 m to 1.10 m.
- Real corridor length after selecting the source remains entirely controlled by the destination/cursor and routing system.
0.43.21 - minimal corridor start and corridor-safe ground rectangles
- Replaces the 1.35 m structure-hover sample corridor with a 0.56 m compact docking/orientation hint; no real corridor route is previewed until the source is selected.
- Reserves 3.45 m per structure side for corridor entrance + docking geometry and uses the expanded rectangle for new structure spacing and edge placement.
- Draws the reserved ground rectangle during placement and at construction step 0.
- Adds a regression based on 0-43-20.gmsave ensuring the old too-close spacing is rejected for new placement and the nearest legal spacing connects with a straight corridor.
0.43.22 - Bio Dome front/back corridor connectivity
- Closes both greenhouse barrel ends with real glazed pressure-envelope caps so generated corridor hatches can attach there.
- Keeps the normal free-angle/on-demand entrance model; no predefined Bio Dome doors are added.
- Adds size/rotation regressions for both long-axis ends and keeps save schema unchanged.

0.43.23 - doubled new-game playfield sizes
- Doubles the New Game default from 64x64 to 128x128.
- Doubles every selectable playfield size: 32..1024 in 32 m steps instead of 16..512 in 16 m steps.
- Debug start template now uses 128x128. Legacy saves as small as 16 remain readable; save validation accepts the new 1024 maximum.

0.43.24 - scrollable upper-left selection/info panel
- Caps the selection/details panel above the build bar and adds mouse-wheel scrolling when its content is taller than the available viewport.
- Adds a proportional scrollbar, per-selection scroll reset/clamping, and prevents panel wheel input from also zooming the camera or changing placement size.
- Adds overflow regression coverage; optimized release validation passes 24/24 registered CTest groups.

0.43.25 - switchable safe solar tracking and Air Lock supply attachment
- Adds a per-Solar Panel SUN TRACKING ON/OFF control in the selected structure status panel. Tracking remains enabled by default; disabling it keeps the collector at its authored default orientation.
- Solar production now respects collector orientation when tracking is disabled, while tracked collectors retain maximum incidence output whenever the star is above the horizon.
- Replaces shortest-arc collector rotation with physical azimuth + tilt tracking so the wide collector head cannot roll through the playfield; adds a final ground-plane penetration guard.
- Save schema 22 persists each Solar Panel tracking setting; older saves load with tracking enabled.
- Supply Channel selection now resolves Air Lock attachment to the permitted longitudinal end, including when hovering the side/center of the Air Lock or snapping from just outside it. Existing one-side-only Air Lock connection rules remain enforced.

0.43.28 -> 0.43.29 : replaced the bundled DEBUG START TEMPLATE payload with the supplied 0-43-28.gmsave layout; debug-template import now uses the current 128x128 template playfield requirement.

0.43.30 - larger power storage and robust scrollable status frame
- Increased every Power Storage capacity by 50%: Small 30 -> 45 kWh and Standard 60 -> 90 kWh.
- Centralized Power Storage capacity so simulation limits, the top power indicator and the selected-structure status frame always report the same value.
- Reworked selection/status-frame overflow sizing to include newer telemetry, warnings and controls before calculating the scroll range; overflowing content remains clipped to the frame and is reachable with the mouse wheel.
- Wheel input over the status frame remains isolated from world-camera zoom, and a proportional scrollbar is shown only when content exceeds the available frame height.

0.43.31 - battery-deadlock recovery and safety-state hardening
- A depleted rover battery is no longer reported as INVALID MOTION BOUNDS. The safety coordinator receives the rover's physical speed capability while energy availability remains an energy/dispatch concern.
- Until the first operational Robot Workshop exists, the starter apron provides a bootstrap charger. At <=10% charge an interruptible rover preserves its mode, target, cargo and logistics reservation, returns to its original starter bay, charges to 80%, then resumes the preserved task.
- Existing saves that already contain a 0 kWh rover receive only the protected 2.5% emergency reserve required to reach charging, preventing a permanent legacy-save deadlock without refilling the pack for normal work.
- Added a battery regression that starts one rover at 0 kWh and one below reserve, verifies both recover without entering SAFETY FAULT, and retains the calibrated no-recharge Stage-1/Stage-2 bootstrap test.
- Replayed 0-43-28(1).gmsave: builder recovery from 0 kWh and carrier recovery from 5.6% both proceed without a fault; the carrier resumes its preserved 10 kg corridor shipment.

0.43.32 - local generator buffers and larger battery storage
- Solar panels now retain up to 4 kWh (standard) / 2 kWh (small) of otherwise-curtailed generation and use it before the main battery for local/network load.
- Water drillers now retain up to 100 L (standard) / 50 L (small) and automatically unload buffered water into connected tanks when capacity becomes available.
- Dedicated Power Storage capacity increased by another 50% to 135 kWh standard / 67.5 kWh small.
- Selection inspector exposes generator buffer fill levels.

0.43.33 - robot enclosure prevention and legacy pocket recovery
- Construction-stage commits now run a full-playfield exterior-connectivity guard so several structures/corridors cannot collectively seal a rover into a closed pocket.
- If a pending stage would disconnect a rover from the protected perimeter service lane, the stage is held and the rover is routed through the still-open escape before geometry becomes solid.
- Legacy saves that already contain an exterior rover in a sealed component receive a one-time emergency extraction to the nearest exterior-connected clear point while preserving cargo, construction/logistics assignment and reservations.
- Replayed 0-43-31.gmsave: the trapped builder at approximately (-45.56, 12.33) is recovered to an exterior-connected point around (-45.70, 18.58) and remains out of SAFETY FAULT during continued simulation.
0.43.37 - persistent carrier return routing and stuck-CPU fix
- Fixed an idle/low-battery Carrier workshop-return deadlock where logistics housekeeping cleared the route every frame immediately before the workshop controller requested the same route again.
- Preserve the Carrier incremental planner while an operational Robot Workshop owns the idle-return goal; real goal changes are still invalidated by the safety coordinator.
- Replayed 0-43-36.gmsave: CR-01 leaves the stuck position around (-56.83, 20.00), reaches and parks at the workshop without SAFETY FAULT, with planner restarts reduced from thousands to a few and node expansions from millions to only a few thousand.
- Added a save-specific regression that requires the Carrier to escape, park, remain fault-free, and stay below bounded planner restart/expansion limits.

